// Phase 36 Plan 36-02 — composer-facing acceptance for the universal
// named-argument call syntax (D-36-11).
//
// Exercises the new `(fn name=value)` surface. Per RESEARCH § Named-arg
// syntax (mirrors Python/C# convention):
//   - positional args MUST precede all named args
//   - a named arg `name=expr` binds the slot whose parameter name is `name`
//   - positional + named args targeting the same slot raises a clear diag
//   - signatures registered WITHOUT `ParameterNames` (the not-yet-backfilled
//     tail in Plans 36-03/04) raise a graceful "does not yet support named
//     arguments" advisory rather than misbehaving (RESEARCH Pitfall 5)
//
// USAGE (Plan 35-04 framework — bodies deferred via lazy):
//   $ dotnet run --project flow-cli -- test tests/test_named_args.flow
//
// At Plan 36-02 commit time, the only builtins that opt into named-arg
// resolution are the ones explicitly annotated in this plan (transpose).
// Plans 36-03 + 36-04 backfill the remaining ~350 builtin signatures with
// ParameterNames so the surface works universally; until then, named-arg
// calls to un-annotated builtins surface the safety-net advisory.

use "@std"
use "@test"

// Bind the input sequence to a variable — embedding `| C4q D4q |` directly
// inside a `(transpose ...)` arg list interleaves note-stream lexing with
// call-arg parsing in unintended ways. The named-arg fact is what we're
// testing; the input is plumbing.
Sequence base_seq = | C4q D4q |

// ---- Test 1: named-arg form matches positional form ----
// `(transpose base_seq amount=2)` and `(transpose base_seq 2)` must produce
// the same result. Phase 36 Plan 36-02 annotates `transpose` with
// ParameterNames so this is the canonical surface for both calls. Use
// `assertNotesMatch` for structural Sequence equality — `assertEq` falls
// back to reference equality for non-primitive Values (Phase 35 LANG-04).
(test "transpose named matches positional"
  lazy((assertNotesMatch (transpose base_seq 2)
                         (transpose base_seq amount=2))))

// ---- Test 2: negative literal after `=` lexes as a single signed token ----
// Phase 26 D-04 expression-start set already includes TokenType.Assign, so
// `arg=-5` parses as a single signed IntLiteral. Exercised through transpose
// (which accepts a negative semitone for transposing down).
(test "transpose accepts negative named amount"
  lazy((assertNotesMatch (transpose base_seq -2)
                         (transpose base_seq amount=-2))))

(print "ALL NAMED-ARG TESTS REGISTERED")
